      "output": "Welcome to the team. Understanding how deadline budgets, retries, cancellation, and idempotency interact is critical for building reliable distributed services. These mechanisms are not independent; they form a cohesive system designed to handle transient failures while preventing resource exhaustion and duplicate side effects.\n\nA **deadline budget** defines the maximum time allowed for a request to complete, including all internal processing and network hops. When a client sets a deadline, it propagates through the service chain. If a downstream service takes too long, the deadline expires, triggering **cancellation**. Cancellation is the mechanism that stops work in progress to free up resources (CPU, memory, connections) immediately. Without cancellation, expired requests would continue consuming resources, leading to cascading failures.\n\n**Retries** are attempts to re-execute a failed operation. However, retries must respect the remaining deadline budget. If a request has 100ms remaining and a retry takes 50ms, you cannot retry twice. Blindly retrying without checking the remaining budget can cause \"retry storms,\" where overloaded services receive exponentially more traffic, worsening the outage. Therefore, retries should be bounded by the remaining time and often include exponential backoff to reduce load.\n\n**Idempotency** ensures that performing an",
